      "text_raw": "Even with the simplest,\nsingle-stage, fully-differen-\ntial amplifier, we have to be\ncareful with the definition of\nthe load capacitance. After\nall, this load capacitance\ndetermines the GBW!\nWith a floating capaci-\ntance (at the left) we have to\ninclude this capacitance\ntwice in the differential\nGBW . Moreover there\nDM\nis no common-mode load\ncapacitance! Its GBW\nCM\nwould therefore be infinity.\nWith two capacitors to\nground, the situation is very different. The differential load capacitance is smaller; it is C itself.\nL\nThe common-mode load capacitance is not at zero. It is twice C !\nL",
      "text": "Even with the simplest, single-stage, fully-differential amplifier, we have to be careful with the definition of the load capacitance. After all, this load capacitance determines the GBW! With a floating capacitance (at the left) we have to include this capacitance twice in the differential GBW . Moreover there DM is no common-mode load capacitance! Its GBW CM would therefore be infinity. With two capacitors to ground, the situation is very different. The differential load capacitance is smaller; it is C itself. L The common-mode load capacitance is not at zero. It is twice C ! L"
    }
